1
00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:07.911
I found the unit architecture useful for
many applications I have worked on, and

2
00:00:07.911 --> 00:00:10.278
there's this one of the most important and

3
00:00:10.278 --> 00:00:14.320
foundational neural network
architectures of computer vision today.

4
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.540
So in this video, let's dig in the details
of exactly how the U-Net works.

5
00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.480
This is what a U-Net looks like.

6
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.054
I know there's a lot going on in this
picture, is okay we'll break it down and

7
00:00:25.054 --> 00:00:27.520
step it through one step at the time.

8
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.554
But I want to quickly show you
the final outcome of what a unit

9
00:00:31.554 --> 00:00:33.430
architecture looks like.

10
00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:36.982
And this, by the way,
is also why it's called a unit,

11
00:00:36.982 --> 00:00:40.781
because when you draw it like this,
it looks a lot like a U.

12
00:00:40.781 --> 00:00:45.231
But let's break it down and
build this back up one piece at a time.

13
00:00:45.231 --> 00:00:49.717
So we're going to make sure we know what
all of the things going on in this diagram

14
00:00:49.717 --> 00:00:50.980
actually are doing.

15
00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:56.144
These ideas were due to Olaf Ranneberger
for the Fisher and Thomas Bronx.

16
00:00:56.144 --> 00:00:59.457
Fun fact,
when they wrote the original unit paper,

17
00:00:59.457 --> 00:01:04.404
they were thinking of the application
of biomedical image segmentation,

18
00:01:04.404 --> 00:01:06.570
really segmenting medical images.

19
00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:11.072
But these ideas turned out to be
useful for many other computer vision,

20
00:01:11.072 --> 00:01:13.840
semantic segmentation
applications as well.

21
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.582
So the input to the unit is an image,
let's say,

22
00:01:17.582 --> 00:01:24.260
is h by w by three, for
three channels RGB channels.

23
00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:30.440
I'm going to visualize this
image as a thin layer like that.

24
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:36.058
I know that previously we had taken
neural network layers and drawn them,

25
00:01:36.058 --> 00:01:42.990
as three D blocks like this, where
this might be rise of h by w by three.

26
00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:46.508
But in order to make
the unit diagram look

27
00:01:46.508 --> 00:01:49.291
simpler going to imagine just
looking at this edge on.

28
00:01:49.291 --> 00:01:51.940
So all you see is this.

29
00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:59.041
And the rest of this is hidden behind this
dark blue rectangle that I've have drawn.

30
00:01:59.041 --> 00:02:01.030
And so that's what this is.

31
00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:06.863
The height of this is, the height h and
the width of this is three, right,

32
00:02:06.863 --> 00:02:10.473
with the number of channels,the depth is 3.

33
00:02:10.473 --> 00:02:12.840
So it looks very thin.

34
00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:17.263
So to simplify the unit diagram, I'm
going to use these rectangles rather than

35
00:02:17.263 --> 00:02:20.640
three D shapes to
illustrate the algorithm.

36
00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.246
Now, the first part of the unit uses
normal feed forward neural network

37
00:02:25.246 --> 00:02:26.880
convolutional layers.

38
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:32.212
So I'm going to use a black arrow
to denote a convolutional layer,

39
00:02:32.212 --> 00:02:35.790
followed by a value activation function.

40
00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:40.501
So the next layer, maybe we'll say that,
we have increased the number of channels

41
00:02:40.501 --> 00:02:44.741
a little bit, but the dimension is still
height by width by a little bit more

42
00:02:44.741 --> 00:02:49.551
channels and then another convolutional
layer with a rather activation function.

43
00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:55.380
Now we're still in the first
half of the neural network.

44
00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:59.760
We're going to use Max pooling
to reduce the height and width.

45
00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.773
So maybe you end up with a set of
activations where the height and

46
00:03:03.773 --> 00:03:09.440
width is lower, but maybe a sticker, so
the number of channels is increasing.

47
00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.660
Then we have two more layers of
normal feet forward convolutions with

48
00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:19.980
a radio activation function, and
then the supply Max pooling again.

49
00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:22.090
And so you get that, right.

50
00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.130
And then you repeat again.

51
00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.740
And so you end up with this.

52
00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:32.148
So, so far, this is the normal convolution
layers with activation functions that

53
00:03:32.148 --> 00:03:37.261
you've been used to from earlier videos
with occasional max pooling layers.

54
00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:44.740
So notice that the height of this
layer I deal with is now very small.

55
00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:49.690
So we're going to start to apply
transpose convolution layers,

56
00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:54.100
which I'm going to note by
the green arrow in order to build

57
00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.620
the dimension of this
neural network back up.

58
00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:02.695
So with the first transpose
convolutional layer or trans conv layer,

59
00:04:02.695 --> 00:04:07.440
you're going to get a set of
activations that looks like that.

60
00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.316
In this example, we did not
increase the height and width, but

61
00:04:12.316 --> 00:04:15.940
we did decrease the number of channels.

62
00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:20.803
But there's one more thing you need to
do to build a unit, which is to add in

63
00:04:20.803 --> 00:04:25.450
that skip connection which I'm going
to denote with this grey arrow.

64
00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:30.393
What the skip connection does is
it takes this set of activations and

65
00:04:30.393 --> 00:04:32.760
just copies it over to the right.

66
00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.160
And so the set of activations
you end up with is like this.

67
00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:40.972
The light blue part comes from
the transpose convolution, and

68
00:04:40.972 --> 00:04:44.980
the dark blue part is just
copied over from the left.

69
00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:49.711
To keep on building up the unit we
are going to then apply a

70
00:04:49.711 --> 00:04:52.927
couple more layers of the regular convolutions,

71
00:04:52.927 --> 00:04:58.555
followed by our value activation
function so denoted by the black arrows,

72
00:04:58.555 --> 00:05:03.780
like so, and then we apply another
transpose convolutional layer.

73
00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:08.809
So green arrow and here we're going
to start to increase the dimension,

74
00:05:08.809 --> 00:05:11.960
increase the height and
width of this image.

75
00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:15.540
And so now the height is getting bigger.

76
00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:18.861
But here, too,
we're going to apply a skip connection.

77
00:05:18.861 --> 00:05:23.969
So there's a grey arrow again where
they take this set of activations and

78
00:05:23.969 --> 00:05:27.161
just copy it right there,
over to the right.

79
00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:32.871
More convolutional layers and other
transpose convolution, skip connection.

80
00:05:32.871 --> 00:05:36.257
Once again, we're going to take
this set of activations and

81
00:05:36.257 --> 00:05:39.849
copy it over to the right and
then more convolutional layers,

82
00:05:39.849 --> 00:05:42.940
followed by another transpose convolution.

83
00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:45.880
Skip connection, copy that over.

84
00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:49.909
And now we're back to a set
of activations that is

85
00:05:49.909 --> 00:05:54.440
the original input images,
height and width.

86
00:05:54.440 --> 00:06:00.958
We're going to have a couple more layers
of a normal fee forward convolutions, and

87
00:06:00.958 --> 00:06:06.076
then finally, to take this and
map this to our segmentation map,

88
00:06:06.076 --> 00:06:11.477
we're going to use a one by one
convolution which I'm going to denote

89
00:06:11.477 --> 00:06:17.651
with that magenta arrow to finally give
us this which is going to be our output.

90
00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:23.889
The dimensions of this output
layer is going to be h by w, so

91
00:06:23.889 --> 00:06:29.740
the same dimensions as our
original input by num classes.

92
00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:34.020
So if you have three classes to try and
recognize, this will be three.

93
00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:37.808
If you have ten different classes
to try to recognize in your

94
00:06:37.808 --> 00:06:41.090
segmentation at then that
last number will be ten.

95
00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:46.139
And so what this does is for every one of
your pixels you have h by w pixels you

96
00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:53.095
have, an array or a vector, essentially
of n classes numbers that tells you for

97
00:06:53.095 --> 00:06:57.067
our pixel how likely is that pixel to come
from each of these different classes.

98
00:06:57.067 --> 00:07:00.095
And if you take a arg max
over these n classes,

99
00:07:00.095 --> 00:07:04.960
then that's how you classify each of
the pixels into one of the classes,

100
00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:10.240
and you can visualize it like
the segmentation map showing on the right.

101
00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:11.040
So that's it.

102
00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:15.455
You've learned about the transpose
convolution and the unit architecture.

